<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mérida, Spain</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mérida is a city on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> river in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, capital of the autonomous community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>. Founded in 25 BCE as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emerita-augusta">Emerita Augusta</a> for veterans of Augustus' wars, it became the capital of Lusitania and keeps one of the largest groups of Roman monuments in Spain. Under Muslim rule it was often a centre of revolt against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, lost ground to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>, and was taken by León in 1230.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/merida-spain.jpg" alt="Mérida, Spain" width="320" height="289" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Fernando · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Temple_of_Diana_in_M%C3%A9rida_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mérida lies 217 metres above sea level in the western-central part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, crossed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a> and Albarregas rivers, in the Province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>. The name comes from the Latin Emerita, 'retired' or 'veteran', and is part of the name Augusta Emerita given to the Roman colony. A hoard of gold jewellery excavated from a girl's grave in 1870, now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>, shows that the site was inhabited in prehistory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Augustus ordered the town founded in 25 BCE to settle discharged soldiers of the V Alaudae and X Gemina. It guarded a strategic pass and the bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadiana">Guadiana</a>, and quickly became one of the most important cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispania">Roman Hispania</a>. It was the capital of Lusitania and in the fourth century of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocese-of-hispania">Diocese of Hispania</a>, and a terminus of the Vía de la Plata, the Roman road linking the gold mines near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturica-augusta">Asturica Augusta</a> with the south. In 409 it became capital of the short-lived Kingdom of the Alans under Attaces, who died in battle against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/visigoths">Visigoths</a> in 418; the city was absorbed into the neighbouring Vandal kingdom and taken by the Visigoths in 469.</p><p>The Umayyad commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musa-ibn-nusayr">Musa ibn Nusayr</a> conquered Mérida in 713, and it became capital of the Cora of Mérida. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> reused and expanded Roman buildings, and the emir Abd ar-Rahman II built the Alcazaba in 835 on the Roman walls.</p><p>In the ninth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozarabs">Mozarabs</a> of Mérida rebelled often against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliphate</a>, which contributed to the city's gradual decline, and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fitna-of-al-andalus">Fitna of al-Andalus</a> it became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taifa">Taifa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/badajoz">Badajoz</a>. Its metropolitan seat passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago-de-compostela">Santiago de Compostela</a> in 1119. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-ix-of-leon">Alfonso IX of León</a> reconquered the city in 1230 and gave it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/order-of-santiago">Order of Santiago</a>. Recovery began in the fifteenth century. Monuments were damaged in the Napoleonic Wars, and Nationalist forces took the city on 10 August 1936 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Mérida is the capital of the autonomous community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/extremadura">Extremadura</a>, a status it received in 1983, and had 60,119 inhabitants in 2017. It preserves more major Roman monuments than any other city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, among them the Roman theatre and amphitheatre, the remains of a circus and the Puente Romano, the longest surviving Roman bridge. Its archaeological site was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1993, and the National Museum of Roman Art was designed by Rafael Moneo.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mérida, Spain” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Abd al-Rahman ibn Marwan, known as al-Jilliqi, 'the Galician', was a muwallad leader from <b>Mérida</b> who rebelled against the Emirate of Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/state/marwanid-badajoz">Marwanid Badajoz</a></li><li><blockquote>After 1009 the freedman Sabur al-Saqlabi took control of the old Lower March around <b>Mérida</b> and Badajoz.</blockquote><a href="/state/taifa-of-badajoz">Taifa of Badajoz</a></li></ul></section>
